// A hundred thousand values through one slot (RFC-0090 M1).
//
// This ran a million cells through Path B's slab of 65,536 and proved that the
// compiler's inferred `release` fired: without it the 65,537th `cell` trapped.
// The proof was a trap that did not happen, and it measured the INFERENCE.
//
// Two of those three things are gone. A `Slots<T>` grows, so there is no bound
// to trip; and a handle is a plain value, so nothing about it is inferred —
// `remove` is written where the program means it. What is left is the property
// the old file was really about: a slot that goes back is a slot that comes out
// again. A hundred thousand inserts and as many removals leave a table of ONE
// slot.
//
// So the number below replaces the absent trap. It is printed, so all three
// engines are held to it, and it fails loudly if reuse ever stops working —
// where the old file could only fail by trapping, and only past 65,536.
//
// The count came down from a million because the interpreter now runs Vyrn for
// what used to be four builtin calls, and a million turns of it took 75 seconds
// where the builtins took 0.8. A hundred thousand is still past the slab that
// bound the old file, and the property under test does not depend on the count.

import { Slots, newSlots, insert, remove, capacity } from "std/slots"

fn main() -> Int64 {
    let mut cells: Slots<Int64> = newSlots()
    let mut i = 0
    let mut last = 0
    while i < 100000 {
        // `insert` takes its payload by `consume`, and movecheck cannot see
        // that this instance's `T` is an `Int64` that owns nothing — so the
        // loop counter is copied into a binding of the turn rather than
        // handed over.
        let v = i
        let h = insert(cells, v)
        cells[h] = cells[h] + 1
        last = cells[h]
        remove(cells, h) // the slot goes back; the next turn takes it
        i = i + 1
    }

    print(last) // 100000
    print(capacity(cells)) // 1 — a hundred thousand values, one slot
    return 0
}
